.TH NETDIFF 1
.SH NAME
netdiff \- compare two netlists by their circuits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netdiff
.I a.nl b.nl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Netdiff
matches the parts of two netlists,
.IR netlist (5),
by their type, value, rating and part number and by how they are
connected, and prints each part and each pin of a net that differs,
one per line.
No output means the circuits are the same.
Designators and the names of nets play no part, except that a global
net matches only the global net of its name, and the class of a net
plays no part.
A net of one pin, unless global, is no connection.
.PP
Values compare as numbers with their units: 499R, 499 and 499\[*W]
agree, as do 1.00K and 1k, or 0.1\[mc]F, 0.1uF, 0.1U, 100N, 100n and 100nF;
ohms, farads and henries go without saying.
The pins of a swap list may trade places.
.PP
The parts are matched by labels refined in turn, after Weisfeiler and
Lehman: a part starts with a hash of its type and values, a net takes
the sum of hashes of its pins (the label of the part and the class of
the pin), and a part the sum of hashes of its nets, until the labels
split no further.
A label held by one unmatched part on each side pairs them; failing
that, one pair of alike parts (as two resistors in parallel) is
matched, and the refining goes on.
Where the circuits differ, labels made with fewer passes, which see
less far, pair the parts near the difference; parts still unmatched
pair by their values where their nets agree best, or are reported.
Each pass is linear in the pins.
The pin records of pins on no net are
.IR schcheck (1)'s
and make no difference.
.SH EXAMPLES
netdiff ours.nl theirs.nl
.br
part only in ours.nl: R12 resistor value 499
.br
net fb / N7: pin only in theirs.nl: R3.2
.SH SEE ALSO
netlist(5), schnet(1), diff(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status 1 if the netlists differ, 2 on a fault in a file.
